  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery looks quite impressive this afternoon, \nshowcasing the omega-like blocking pattern over the CONUS with deep \nmidlevel lows off the Mid-Atlantic and the Pacific Northwest and \nstout ridging over the central CONUS. Will note an embedded \nshortwave moving over the Plains, which could impacts the UP later \nthis weekend...but more on that later. In the meantime, visible \nsatellite shows mostly sunny skies over the  UP, with just some \npatchy fair weather cumulus. Under sunny skies, temperatures are \nrising quite nicely into the lower 70s for most. With dewpoints \nmixing out, much of the interior UP is already seeing RH falling to \naround 45-50%, though some spots are falling even further to \nnear/below 40%. Still, light winds only peaking at around 5-10mph \nshould limit fire weather concerns. Temperatures quickly fall back \ninto the lower to mid 40s across most of the area tonight, while \nsoundings support a chance for patchy fog into the early morning \nhours. \n\nHigh pressure will remain over the Great Lakes through the weekend \nas the blocking pattern persists over the CONUS. In terms of \nsensible weather this will more or less maintain the status quo \nacross the UP through early next week, yielding continued dry \nweather with highs in the upper 60s to low 70s each afternoon. The \naforementioned weak shortwave riding the western periphery of the \nridge this weekend could bring a few showers to the far western UP \nlate Saturday into Sunday, but NBM continues to cap chances for \nmeasurable precipitation around 20-30% near Ironwood. Will need to \nkeep an eye on minimum RH values over the next few days with \nseasonable warm temps and good afternoon dewpoint mixing, but weak \npressure gradient and light winds will continue to limit fire \nweather concerns.\n\nHigh pressure finally breaks down Monday into Tuesday, paving the \nway for more substantive troughing to move into the region during \nthe middle of the week. There is still quite a bit of variability \nwith regards to the details ranging from strength, timing, and \norientation of the trough to the potential for a stronger surface \nlow to lift out of the Plains and track close to the area. Despite \nthe model spread, there is broad agreement on more widespread rain \nreturning to the region Tuesday into Wednesday. NBM currently \ndepicts a 30-40% chance for at least a half inch of rain across much \nof the UP during this time period.",
